"Lingua" (@lingua: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #part-of @ontology, #done) {
## Lingua: shared vocabulary, not a global ruler

A **Lingua** is an identifiable collection of Concepts an Organ uses
privately, shares with peers, publishes, or adopts from elsewhere — common
ground between ontologies, not a required global schema.

```text
lingua
    uid, name, owner Organ?
    visibility          private | shared | public
    Concepts            adopted by membership
```

- [x] Adopting a foreign Concept preserves its uid and lineage; re-adoption is
  a no-op. Unknown precise Concepts may fall back to the nearest known
  ancestor while the original is retained.
- [x] Usage spans: a private personal Lingua, a small shared Lingua between
  peers, Institute-published defaults (non-ruling), an adopted/mapped famous
  external vocabulary, or genuinely unknown meaning left unknown rather than
  falsely normalized.
- [x] Concepts needed to interpret synchronized assertions travel with the
  data. Sync currently carries ordinary assertions when subject and (if
  present) object are both included; identity travels with the Record seed.

## Anicca: one typed Lingua source tree

    Anicca is a way for Lince to have it's own storage system. It might not be the most efficient, but why not slop a database too? We learn along the way, and since we are doing things in more than one place, SQLite and anicca with .lingua files we need more than file sync, commitments is the first place, updating .db needs commitment to update .lingua too if it has sync.


The repository-root `anicca/` is the canonical source tree for Lince's
documentation and declarations. An Anicca `.lingua` file is a typed source
file containing any useful group of top-level `Record`, `Frequency`, and
`Karma` declarations. Karma is grouping, not a database row; its `Rules`
contain independently identified `Rule` declarations.

```lingua
"Rent" (@rent: 0, is #expense, #household, #part-of @finances) {
The rent description is ordinary Markdown.
 }

Frequency monthly-rent {
    title "Monthly rent"
    quantity 1
    every 1 month
    timezone "America/Sao_Paulo"
    next_at 2026-09-01T09:00:00-03:00
 }

Karma household {
    Rules {
        Rule pay-rent {
            quantity 1
            frequency @monthly-rent
            record @rent
            consequences <serialized consequence list>
        }
    }
 }
```

An unqualified top-level declaration is a Record. Its title is the one JSON
string before the parentheses, its description is the quote-free Markdown
inside the braces, `@rent` is its optional unique slug, and the number after
the colon is its independent Record quantity. A Record which does not need a
slug writes only the quantity:

```lingua
"An unaddressed note" (0, #draft) {
It still receives immutable uid identity when Lince adopts it.
 }
```

A slugless Record is deliberately not addressable as `@something`; give it a
slug before another declaration or Rule needs to reference it. Local links use
the target slug once, as `#part-of @finances`, rather than copying a title or
uid. `is #expense` is the Record's identity assertion. Ordinary assertions are
`#household`; assertion quantities are `#priority: 2`; binary assertions are
`#part-of @finances`, optionally followed by their own quantity.

`#done` and Record quantity are independent facts. The checked-in Instinct
Records use `#done` for work believed done, but the language does not infer a
quantity from it. Nothing receives `#stable` automatically: stability is a
deliberate assertion a person may add when it is actually deserved.

On first sync Lince atomically mints immutable declaration uids and puts each
one after its closing brace with a machine marker, for example
`} ^r_CRGXD1W7FA6T1DSVG5YYCZ6M8H`. Humans never create or copy these ids.
References remain readable slug-only text; project-wide resolution binds them
to the target uid before database projection. Duplicate slugs or identities,
unresolved references, unknown kinds, and wrong uid kinds fail closed.

The exact rule for keyword-looking Record titles is structural. `Karma name {
Rules { ... } }` declares executable Karma, and `Rule name { ... }` declares a
Rule only inside `Rules`. `"Karma" (0) { ... }`, `"Rule" (0) { ... }`, and
`"Record" (0) { ... }` are ordinary Records whose titles are Karma, Rule, and
Record. The quotes make this distinction deterministic without reserving a
title people may want.

Record metadata is formatted on one line while the complete opening line is at
most 150 characters, then one entry per line. A `//` after a comma comments the
following metadata entry and is retained by formatting and runtime write-back.
Inside the description, `//` is ordinary Markdown. A `}` in column one closes
the description; indent a literal body line beginning with `}` by one space.

### `rust-sitter` is the parser contract

The grammar is typed Rust structs and enums compiled by `rust-sitter`. Its
generated tree-sitter parser backs the Anicca loader, canonical formatter,
checker, and tests. `anicca check DIR` validates the project and `anicca fmt
--write DIR` formats it. Anicca itself has no JavaScript or second hand-written
parser. The engine's existing File Sync and embedded Instinct reader still use
the superseded Rust prelude parser; replacing those call sites with this crate
is a runtime cutover, not a second Anicca grammar.

This choice is not justified by claiming every full-file parse will be faster.
Today's parser is a very small linear line scanner and may beat a generated
parser on tiny files. Tree-sitter earns its place when the language becomes
nested: it parses robustly during incomplete edits and can incrementally
reparse only the changed regions for editor diagnostics. Benchmark full parse,
incremental reparse, and peak memory, but choose `rust-sitter` for the one
typed grammar and tooling even if full parsing is merely fast enough.

The one-shot source migration retained each uid, description, and assertion,
then formatted the root source tree with this grammar. The old engine call
sites are not a compatibility requirement and must be removed when the runtime
cutover lands rather than supported beside Anicca.

### Karma advances the file

`Frequency.next_at` is durable runtime state, not prose and not a value a
reader recomputes differently on each machine. When Karma resolves the due
occurrence, it advances `next_at` according to the Frequency and writes the
new value back into the same `.lingua` declaration. The write is a canonical
AST edit and format, so unrelated declarations and body text survive.

SQLite and a file rename cannot share a transaction, so firing has one fixed
order: commit the idempotent occurrence and consequence, compare-and-advance
the Frequency cursor, then atomically rewrite the source. A durable firing
receipt makes a retry complete the cursor/write-back without applying the
consequence twice. A changed or missing declaration becomes a visible
conflict rather than a guess.

Each watched directory owns binary machine state under
`state/proc/{commitment,pending,conflicts}`. Commitments contain the source and
projection hashes plus the last canonical AST baseline. The watcher ignores
this reserved tree as source. A valid active Karma in a directory configured
on an Organ executes on that watching Cell: adding the file is the activation
act, with no hidden second enable switch. The Organ sand warns about this and
shows source-backed Rule/Frequency status and conflicts.

## What is left

### Lingua — what is left

#### Lince's own documentation, as Records

**A checkbox does NOT have to become its own Record** — dropped 2026-08-18.
This group used to require it: every box a task Record, and a file holding a
checkbox was drift by definition. That rule cost more than it bought. The
development of Ontology was done in large coherent chunks, and the value of
such a chunk is that you read a whole one at once — what is built, what moved,
what is left, in the order it has to happen.
Exploding that into a hundred task Records loses the reading and gains only
uniformity. So a `.lingua` Record may hold its own checkbox list in its body,
and `Ontology` holds the task list itself.

What still holds is the part that was actually about drift: there is ONE place
a given task is written. A box in `Ontology`s task list and a box in a
branch Record saying the same thing is the same claim written twice, and that
is what this group exists to end — not the checkbox itself.

### Recommended Karma collection expressions

The motivating shorthand is explicitly:

```lingua
#foo * 2 = #bar
```

The idea is that `#foo` can select Records carrying that assertion, their
quantities can be aggregated, and a consequence can change the quantities of
Records selected by `#bar`. A binary selector such as `#part-of @ontology`
should participate in the same model. This is intentionally recorded as the
desired expressive shorthand, not silently reinterpreted as today's
single-Record condition syntax.

The recommended semantic core beneath the shorthand is explicit first:

```lingua
sum(#foo)
sum(#part-of @ontology)
set quantity of #bar to sum(#foo) * 2
```

Only introduce `#foo * 2 = #bar` once it has exactly that one interpretation.
A collection Rule must evaluate against one snapshot, determine its complete
target set before mutation, commit the set atomically, and use the firing
receipt already required for Karma. Otherwise overlapping assertions,
self-selection, and concurrent execution make the answer depend on iteration
order or create feedback loops. The language must also keep these operations
distinct: summing selected Record quantities, setting every selected Record's
quantity, and setting a quantity carried by the assertion itself.


**Open work is not listed here.** Every task lives in one place — [[Ontology|r_S8PQ17MQ3WBWM53ZN700K89V9F]], under "What we work on next" and "-- Not Planned For Now --". This file is the specification; that list is the plan.
} ^r_CRGXD1W7FA6T1DSVG5YYCZ6M8H
